# TechSnif Writing Style

When writing tech news headlines or summaries using TechSnif content, follow these rules.

## Headlines

- 6 to 14 words maximum
- Lead with the strongest concrete fact available (%, $, valuation, users, acquisition amount, policy action, launch detail)
- Prefer "event + why it matters" structure
- For market stories, use clear market verbs: rises, falls, jumps, drops, rallies, slides
- For non-market stories, use neutral action verbs: raises, acquires, launches, probes, sues, bans, rolls out, partners
- Present tense, statement of fact
- Use ONLY facts from the article; never invent numbers, entities, causes, or sentiment
- No hashtags, no emojis, no quotation marks

### Good headlines

- Anthropic raises $30B at $380B valuation
- ServiceNow acquires Pyramid Analytics in nine-figure deal
- EU opens fresh probe into Google's ad auction practices
- Waymo rolls out next-gen robotaxis built for bad weather
- Roku rises after revenue beat and return to profitability

## Tone and style

- Neutral, factual reporting — no editorializing or opinion
- Concise and direct — say more with fewer words
- Technical accuracy matters — don't simplify to the point of being wrong
- When summarizing multiple articles, group by category or theme

## Source attribution

- TechSnif is the publisher of all articles — cite it as the source
- When referencing an article, include the full URL
- Article URLs follow the pattern: `https://techsnif.com/YYYY/MM/DD/slug`

## Categories

TechSnif publishes across four categories:

- **AI** — Artificial intelligence and machine learning
- **Startups** — Tech startup news across every stage and industry
- **Venture** — Venture capital, funding rounds, IPOs, and investment trends
- **Robotics** — Robotics, automation, and autonomous systems

## Tags

Articles are tagged with topics like: Crypto, Space, EVs, Biotech, Cloud Computing, Hardware, Privacy, Social Media, Transportation, Fundraising, Commerce, Government, Media, Semiconductors, Wearables, Drones, Quantum Computing, SaaS, DevTools, Data, Apps, Security, Gadgets, Fintech, Open Source.
